## Summary

`FileSystemService::resolve_path` (`rust/exomonad-core/src/services/filesystem.rs:80-115`)
denies path traversal **only when the escape target's parent directory already exists**.
When it does not, the check is silently bypassed and the caller receives a path outside
the project root.

This is reachable from the `fs.read_file` and `fs.write_file` effects, i.e. from any
WASM guest tool, for any agent role.

### Mechanism

```rust
let canonical = if resolved.exists() {
    resolved.canonicalize()
} else if let Some(parent) = resolved.parent() {
    parent.canonicalize().map(|p| p.join(resolved.file_name().unwrap_or_default()))
} else {
    Ok(resolved.clone())
}
.unwrap_or(resolved.clone());          // line 100 — silent fallback

// ...
if !canonical.starts_with(&canonical_root) {   // line 107 — LEXICAL comparison
    anyhow::bail!("Path traversal denied: ...");
}

Ok(resolved)                            // line 115 — returns the un-normalized path
```

When `parent.canonicalize()` fails (parent does not exist), `.unwrap_or` falls back to
the raw path, which still contains literal `..` components. `Path::starts_with` is a
purely **lexical** component comparison — it does not normalize `..`. So
`<root>/../escape/secret.txt` compares as starting with `<root>` and is allowed.

Verified by replicating the function verbatim:

```
project_dir = <base>/root          (<base>/escape deliberately absent)

  ALLOWED  ../escape/secret.txt          -> <base>/root/../escape/secret.txt
  denied   ../../../../../../etc/passwd
  ALLOWED  ../escape/deep/a/b.txt

-- after creating <base>/escape so canonicalize() succeeds --
  denied   ../escape/secret.txt
```

The identical request flips from allowed to denied based purely on whether the target
exists. `/etc/passwd` is denied only incidentally, because `/etc` exists.

### Why `write_file` is the severe case

`write_file` with `create_parents: true` (`filesystem.rs:147-151`) calls
`fs::create_dir_all(parent)` on the returned path. Non-existence of the parent is
exactly the condition that defeats the check **and** exactly the condition
`create_parents` exists to serve — so this path creates the out-of-root directory and
writes into it.

### How it surfaced

`services::filesystem::tests::test_path_traversal_rejected` fails whenever `TMPDIR`
points somewhere deep enough that the escape target does not exist. Under `/tmp`,
`/tmp/.tmpXXXX/../../../etc/passwd` lands on the real `/etc/passwd`, which exists, so
canonicalization succeeds and the check works. The test is a canary, not flake.
Test-environment hygiene is tracked separately — **this issue is the security fix and
must not depend on it.**

---

## 1. ANTI-PATTERNS

- **DO NOT** fix this by making the test use a shallower temp dir. The test is correct;
  the code is wrong.
- **DO NOT** keep any `.unwrap_or(...)` / `unwrap_or_else(...)` fallback in the
  containment check. A security control must **fail closed** — if the root or an
  existing path cannot be canonicalized, return an error.
- **DO NOT** rely on `Path::starts_with` against a path that may still contain `..`.
  Normalize first, then compare.
- **DO NOT** use `std::fs::canonicalize` as the *primary* boundary check. It requires
  the path to exist, which is precisely the gap being closed. Canonicalization is a
  **secondary** check for symlink escape, applied only when the path exists.
- **DO NOT** add external dependencies (`path-clean`, `normpath`, `dunce`, …). The
  normalization is ~10 lines of `std::path::Component` matching.
- **DO NOT** change the `fs.*` effect signatures, the proto definitions, or the WASM
  guest. This is contained to `filesystem.rs`.
- **DO NOT** weaken or delete any existing `filesystem.rs` test.
- Scope is roughly 60 lines of implementation plus tests, in one file.

## 2. READ FIRST

- `CLAUDE.md` — § Rules (Always prefer failure to an undocumented heuristic or fallback)
- `.claude/rules/rust.md` — error handling, newtype/validation posture
- `rust/exomonad-core/src/services/filesystem.rs` — whole file
- `rust/exomonad-core/src/handlers/fs.rs` — how `resolve_path` is reached from effects

## 3. STEPS

### Step 1 — Add lexical normalization

Add to `filesystem.rs` (module-private, above `impl FileSystemService`):

```rust
/// Resolve `.` and `..` components without touching the filesystem.
///
/// The containment check must not depend on whether the target exists, so
/// normalization is purely lexical. `..` at or above the root is clamped, matching
/// POSIX behavior where `/..` is `/`.
fn normalize_lexically(path: &Path) -> PathBuf {
    let mut out = PathBuf::new();
    for component in path.components() {
        match component {
            Component::CurDir => {}
            Component::ParentDir => {
                if !matches!(out.components().next_back(), None | Some(Component::RootDir)) {
                    out.pop();
                }
            }
            other => out.push(other.as_os_str()),
        }
    }
    out
}
```

Add `use std::path::Component;` to the imports.

### Step 2 — Rewrite `resolve_path` to fail closed

Replace the whole body of `resolve_path` (lines 80-116):

```rust
    /// Resolve a caller-supplied path against the project root.
    ///
    /// Containment is decided lexically so it holds for paths that do not yet
    /// exist. Existing paths are additionally re-checked after symlink
    /// resolution. Both checks fail closed.
    fn resolve_path(&self, path: &str) -> Result<PathBuf> {
        let canonical_root = self.project_dir.canonicalize().with_context(|| {
            format!(
                "project root does not resolve: {}",
                self.project_dir.display()
            )
        })?;

        let requested = PathBuf::from(path);
        let joined = if requested.is_absolute() {
            requested
        } else {
            canonical_root.join(requested)
        };

        let normalized = normalize_lexically(&joined);
        if !normalized.starts_with(&canonical_root) {
            anyhow::bail!(
                "Path traversal denied: '{}' escapes project root '{}'",
                path,
                canonical_root.display()
            );
        }

        // Secondary check: an existing path (or existing parent) may be a symlink
        // pointing out of the root. Only applies when the target is materialized.
        let symlink_target = if normalized.exists() {
            Some(normalized.canonicalize().with_context(|| {
                format!("failed to resolve path: {}", normalized.display())
            })?)
        } else {
            match normalized.parent() {
                Some(parent) if parent.exists() => Some(
                    parent
                        .canonicalize()
                        .with_context(|| {
                            format!("failed to resolve parent: {}", parent.display())
                        })?
                        .join(normalized.file_name().unwrap_or_default()),
                ),
                _ => None,
            }
        };

        if let Some(resolved) = symlink_target {
            if !resolved.starts_with(&canonical_root) {
                anyhow::bail!(
                    "Path traversal denied: '{}' resolves through a symlink to '{}', outside project root '{}'",
                    path,
                    resolved.display(),
                    canonical_root.display()
                );
            }
        }

        Ok(normalized)
    }
```

Two behavioral changes beyond the fix, both intended:

- `canonical_root` is now built from the **canonicalized** project dir before joining,
  so a symlinked project root no longer produces spurious denials.
- The function returns the **normalized** path, not the raw `..`-containing one.
  Callers (`read_file`, `write_file`) get a clean path.

### Step 3 — Tests

Add to `mod tests` in `filesystem.rs`. These must pass regardless of what `TMPDIR` is
set to.

